<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The ATAS adapter.

    Two build modes:

    1. STUB MODE (the default, and what CI runs here)
         dotnet build -c Release
       Compiles against ApiStub/AtasApiAssumptions.cs, a written-down set of
       ASSUMPTIONS about the ATAS API. This type-checks the adapter. It does NOT
       validate the assumptions. See docs/ATAS-API-VERIFICATION.md.

    2. REAL MODE (on a Windows machine with ATAS installed)
         dotnet build -c Release -p:UseRealAtas=true -p:AtasInstallDir="C:\Program Files (x86)\ATAS Platform"
       Excludes the stub and references the real assemblies. Targets whatever
       $(AtasTargetFrameworks) resolves to, which defaults to the runtime the
       currently installed products were measured on — see the target framework
       note below.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseRealAtas Condition="'$(UseRealAtas)' == ''">false</UseRealAtas>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    REAL-MODE TARGET FRAMEWORKS — dual-platform, selected from measurement.

    The runtime is a property of the INSTALLATION, and it moves. Read it; never
    infer it.

      ATAS Classic  OFT.Platform.runtimeconfig.json
      ATAS X        OFT.PlatformX.runtimeconfig.json

    Two signals are authoritative: the "tfm" in that runtimeconfig, and the
    TargetFrameworkAttribute on the ATAS assemblies themselves. The assembly
    VERSION label is not a signal. Measured 2026-09-12: ATAS.Indicators reports
    identity 8.0.14.399 on Classic and 8.0.15.643 on ATAS X, yet both assemblies
    are .NETCoreApp,Version=v10.0 and both products run net10.0.

    CURRENT MEASURED STATE (Windows machine, 2026-09-12, both products):

      ATAS Classic  runtimeconfig tfm net10.0   assemblies .NETCoreApp v10.0
      ATAS X        runtimeconfig tfm net10.0   assemblies .NETCoreApp v10.0

    So the default is net10.0-windows. It is COMPILE-VERIFIED against the real
    assemblies of both installed products: with .NET SDK 10.0.400, this exact
    reference set and -warnaserror, Classic and ATAS X each built EXIT=0 with 0
    warnings. It is NOT runtime-verified — neither product has loaded the
    recorder yet.

    net8.0-windows is RETAINED as an explicit compatibility target for older,
    net8-based ATAS installations:

      -p:AtasTargetFrameworks=net8.0-windows

    It is not claimed to work with either currently installed product, and it
    demonstrably does not: against these net10 assemblies it fails CS1705
    (ATAS.Indicators uses System.Runtime 10.0.0.0, higher than the referenced
    8.0.0.0). The retention is deliberate rather than nostalgic — Classic on
    this very machine measured .NETCoreApp v8.0 at 2026-09-12T01:35Z and had
    moved to v10.0 by that afternoon, so net8 installations exist in the field
    and the platform demonstrably migrates underneath an install.

    For an explicit multitarget list, escape the semicolon: MSBuild otherwise
    splits an unescaped one into a second switch and fails MSB1006.

      "-p:AtasTargetFrameworks=net8.0-windows%3Bnet10.0-windows"

    HISTORICAL, kept because it explains the shape of this file: real mode once
    targeted net472. That could not legally consume the ATAS assemblies as they
    were then measured, .NETCoreApp,Version=v8.0, and failed CS0012, CS0115 and
    CS0534. ATAS was never the .NET Framework host that configuration assumed.
    The framework family, not the version, was the error there.

    Why -windows rather than plain net10.0: resolving ATAS.Indicators metadata
    demonstrably requires PresentationCore, PresentationFramework, WindowsBase
    and System.Xaml — the same requirement that broke the API probe — and
    ATAS.Indicators.Indicator carries WPF types in its own member signatures
    while this adapter derives from it. A deliberate superset: a missing WPF
    reference fails the build, an unnecessary one does not.

    LIMITATION worth stating plainly: a -windows TFM produces a Windows-only
    assembly. That is correct for ATAS X ON WINDOWS, which is what is being
    validated. If ATAS X on Linux or macOS is ever required, that needs a
    non-Windows TFM with UseWPF off, and whether the ATAS reference assemblies
    permit it is unverified.

    Platform is left at the SDK default (AnyCPU). Nothing here is bitness
    sensitive and ATAS guidance for ordinary custom indicators is AnyCPU.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AtasTargetFrameworks Condition="'$(AtasTargetFrameworks)' == ''">net10.0-windows</AtasTargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks Condition="'$(UseRealAtas)' == 'true'">$(AtasTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
    <TargetFramework Condition="'$(UseRealAtas)' != 'true'">netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF Condition="'$(UseRealAtas)' == 'true'">true</UseWPF>
    <LangVersion>9.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <AssemblyName>NFMarketReplayRecorder.ATAS</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>NFMarketReplayRecorder.ATAS</RootNamespace>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Stub mode -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(UseRealAtas)' != 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);STUB_ATAS</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Real mode: the stub must not exist in the compilation at all -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseRealAtas)' == 'true'">
    <Compile Remove="ApiStub/**/*.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    No explicit System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations reference. It was required
    only because the project targeted net472, where it is a separate framework
    assembly. Under .NET 8 and later the Display and Range attributes come from
    the shared framework and an explicit Reference is obsolete.
  -->

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(UseRealAtas)' == 'true'">
    <Reference Include="ATAS.Indicators">
      <HintPath>$(AtasInstallDir)\ATAS.Indicators.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="ATAS.DataFeedsCore">
      <HintPath>$(AtasInstallDir)\ATAS.DataFeedsCore.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="Utils.Common">
      <HintPath>$(AtasInstallDir)\Utils.Common.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
    <Reference Include="OFT.Attributes">
      <HintPath>$(AtasInstallDir)\OFT.Attributes.dll</HintPath>
      <Private>false</Private>
    </Reference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../NFMarketReplayRecorder.Core/NFMarketReplayRecorder.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
